WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.727
Narrator: Whether you need a snow storm,

00:00:01.727 --> 00:00:05.760
a huge gust of wind,
or a fake rain shower,

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.480
this man's got you covered.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.358
Some TV shows and movies film
scenes in extreme weather

00:00:10.358 --> 00:00:13.090
to make the story feel more realistic.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:15.970
But there's one big
problem with that approach.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.870
Jeremy Chernick: You can
never count on the weather

00:00:17.878 --> 00:00:19.253
to work for you.

00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:20.910
Narrator: Jeremy Chernick is a designer

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.755
at J&amp;M Special Effects in Brooklyn.

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:24.697
Chernick: You can't
schedule the entire crew 

00:00:24.697 --> 00:00:26.080
around waiting for snow.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.163
Narrator: So you often
have to make it yourself.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.620
Jeremy's company creates
practical special effects

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.090
for a variety of live shows, music videos,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.180
and other productions.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.688
Chernick: All the Disney Broadway shows,

00:00:35.688 --> 00:00:37.620
"Aladdin," "Frozen."

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:39.430
Narrator: He made it rain on Shawn Mendes

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.020
at the 2018 MTV VMA's.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.540
J&amp;M also rents out equipment for TV shows

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.480
like "Gotham," and "Elementary."

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.620
Basically, any conditions you can imagine,

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.020
he can whip up.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.990
But the trick to creating fake weather

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.820
is that it just needs to look
real in the camera frame.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.660
Chernick: You don't need
all the weather in the world

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.620
to know it's raining in a shot this big.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.140
You just need rain in the
background or rain on top.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.499
Narrator: So what exactly does it take

00:01:05.508 --> 00:01:08.677
to create the illusion of
weather on camera or on stage?

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.461
Chernick: There are a
lot of different ways

00:01:15.461 --> 00:01:16.703
that rain is done.

00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:19.510
And it depends on whether
you're interior or exterior.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.720
Narrator: Outside, you
can get a permit to hook

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.130
up a hose to a fire
hydrant or a water truck.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.568
Chernick: You can split
that water out to a variety

00:01:26.568 --> 00:01:30.220
of locations, and usually high big towers

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:35.090
go up that can spray water
from unbelievably high.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.840
Narrator: Indoors you
need a self-contained

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.990
system attached to water supply.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:41.226
Chernick: Right above me is a rain bar.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.480
So we have rain that
is pouring down in sort

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.380
of a five-inch channel, so
if you're looking dead at it,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.548
it looks pretty three-dimensional
because you have

00:01:53.548 --> 00:01:58.020
rain here and here, and
plus we can add a level

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.950
of sort of mist into that
that will just give it

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.950
an even larger depth of field.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.400
And you can rows and
rows and rows of them.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.475
The systems in place to keep
that rain from never ever

00:02:10.475 --> 00:02:13.660
dripping unless you wanted
it to rain is actually

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.833
more complex than the moments
when it's actually raining.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.010
It's mostly fairly low tech.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.920
Narrator: There are a
number of different products

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.860
that are used to replicate snowflakes.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.860
Chernick: You can get
pretty large paper flakes

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.170
that are squares and they
fall in a very beautiful

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.033
and specific way, you can
get shredded paper that falls

00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:38.650
sort of organically.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.810
You can get it shredded
plastic, which has a different

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.200
feel to it, and the way
that you deliver that

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.090
is through blowers that
are blowing it far overhead

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.210
and a long distance.

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.210
Narrator: They also have
special soap-based products

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.610
that won't stain clothes or
make the ground slippery.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.820
Chernick: Essentially,
it's like tiny bits of foam

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.123
and that is a machine
that is pushing that soap

00:03:00.123 --> 00:03:05.123
through a filter that
makes it into snowflakes.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.970
Environmentally, there's
all sorts of stuff

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:16.050
that ends up in the air to fill a picture

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.924
in terms of haze, fog, smoke.

00:03:20.924 --> 00:03:23.160
Narrator: These effects
are usually produced

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.070
with a fog machine.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.850
Chernick: The most commonly
used interior fog is

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.690
glycol based, which is a type of alcohol.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.370
Narrator: Sometimes you
want the fog to fill

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.910
the screen, and other times
you want low-lying fog,

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:39.000
which requires a few special tricks.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.130
Chernick: That is most often
done now using liquid CO2,

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.766
or liquid nitrogen to chill the glycol fog

00:03:44.766 --> 00:03:47.240
to a very cold level.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:50.483
And that coldness is what
heats it low to the ground.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:56.230
The thing that's funny about wind, to me,

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:58.280
is that you don't see
wind unless it's either

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.910
moving clothing or hair, or if it has

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.550
some fog or some level of fog in it.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.668
Narrator: J&amp;M has fans of all sizes.

00:04:05.668 --> 00:04:09.010
We have incredibly quiet fans designed

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.430
specifically for live television.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:12.790
Narrator: With any of
these weather effects,

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:14.620
you want to be extra careful to protect

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.600
camera equipment and the actors.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.040
Chernick: There's a shield,
there's plastic bagging,

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:20.305
there's sometimes tents.

00:04:20.305 --> 00:04:22.747
Narrator: There's also a
risk of flooding indoors.

00:04:22.747 --> 00:04:25.520
Chernick: Often scenery is
going to be built and designed

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.160
with water catch underneath,
so it will be raining

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.420
on what looks like the floor,

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.410
but it's designed to collect water.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:32.900
Narrator: While these weather systems

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.350
may not be exactly like the real thing,

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:36.720
they get the job done.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.266
Chernick: You don't have to
do a huge amount of post work

00:04:39.266 --> 00:04:42.440
if your special effects are
right and they look right.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:43.920
And they're telling your story.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.170
And the realistic stuff,
is you can tell it's real.

